{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>This is the book of the generations of man.</b> The Ramban explained that it refers back to the entire Torah, for all of it is 'the book of the generations of man.' And Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra explained that 'the book,' that is, wisdom, is the principal generations of man. And it appears to me that 'this book' refers to what is stated: ״בְּיוֹם בְּרוֹא אֱלֹהִים אָדָם בִּדְמוּת אֱלֹהִים עָשָׂה אוֹתוֹ״ ('On the day that Elohim created man, in the likeness of Elohim He made him'), for on the day that the first man was created he was perfect in formation with all human excellences and was free from every blemish and deficiency. Just as a craftsman writes in a book all the things he needs for his craft, so the Holy One, blessed be He, wrote in a book all the excellences that were in man, by virtue of which he was 'in the likeness of Elohim' — in order to teach the generations how through their sin they brought evil upon themselves, that all the generations went on diminishing from those perfections. As it is written (Psalms 139:16): ״גָלְמִי רָאוּ עֵינֶיךָ וְעַל סִפְרְךָ כֻּלָּם יִכָּתֵבוּ יָמִים יֻצָּרוּ וְלֹא אֶחָד בָּהֶם״ ('Your eyes saw my unformed substance, and in Your book they were all written; days were fashioned, and not one among them'). And our Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted this verse (Sanhedrin 38b) regarding the creation of the first man, and following their approach I say that this is its meaning: 'Your eyes saw my unformed substance' — that it appeared good in the eyes of the Holy One, blessed be He. 'And in Your book they were all written' — this is 'the book of the generations of man,' upon which are written all the human excellences, the totality of which Scripture comprises in saying 'in the likeness of Elohim He created him.' However, 'days were fashioned, and not one among them' — that the days would come when among those born there would not be even one of all those excellences, for they would go on diminishing from that perfection. Therefore 'how weighty also are Your thoughts to me, O El' — those who are friends to the Blessed Holy One and are in the likeness of Elohim, they are precious in my eyes for they are not to be found.'" "Another matter: that all this speaks of human free choice. For 'everything is in the hands of Heaven except the fear of Heaven,' and all of them are the deeds of man, which are called תּוֹלְדוֹת ('generations') and קוֹרוֹת ('occurrences') — it is as though the Holy One, blessed be He, begat them, for all is from Him, blessed be He: wisdom, wealth, might, and length of days. However, 'this is the book,' that is, the good deeds whose source is from within the book — those are the generations of man attributed to man himself, and the Holy One, blessed be He, has no portion in them. For the Holy One, blessed be He, delivered all the deeds of man into his hand, and HaShem did not do all this; man was made master of free choice — ״בְּיוֹם בְּרֹא אֱלֹהִים אָדָם בִּדְמוּת אֱלֹהִים עָשָׂה אוֹתוֹ״ ('On the day that Elohim created man, in the likeness of Elohim He made him'): just as the Holy One, blessed be He, is sovereign over all His deeds, so is man sovereign over all his deeds, and from this aspect he is 'in the likeness of Elohim.' And this is what it says: ״גָלְמִי רָאוּ עֵינֶיךָ וְעַל סִפְרְךָ כֻּלָּם יִכָּתֵבוּ״ (Psalms 139:16), as they said, of blessed memory, in tractate Niddah (16b), that they bring the drop before the Holy One, blessed be He, and say: this child to be born — will he be wise or foolish, weak or mighty, rich or poor? But 'righteous or wicked' they do not say, because everything is in the hands of Heaven except the fear of Heaven. This is what it says: 'Your eyes saw my unformed substance' — before my formation, the Holy One, blessed be He, saw what would be in my unformed substance when they set the drop before Him. 'And in Your book they were all written' — that they write in a book all his circumstances, whether he will be mighty or weak, and so forth. And even 'days were fashioned' — they apportion him how long he will live. However, 'and not one among them' — for one thing is missing from the book of perfection: 'righteous or wicked' they did not say. Therefore it was not written in the book upon which they write what the child's destiny and deeds will be. Consequently, 'how weighty also are Your thoughts to me, O El' — those who were made 'in the likeness of Elohim,' sovereigns over all their deeds, as stated."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The closing citation continues Psalm 139:17; the Kli Yakar quotes only the opening words ״מַה יָּקְרוּ רֵעֶיךָ אֵל״ which might more commonly read ״מַה יָּקְרוּ רֵעֶיךָ אֱלֹהִים״, but following the voice rule the divine name אֵל is transliterated as El."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:21.034530571Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}